{
  "id": "the-broken-window",
  "title": "The Broken Window",
  "level": "B1",
  "summary": "The ball went through the window. Now Kerem and Ayse have to decide what to do.",
  "teachingMethod": "Drama-based",
  "teachingMethodReason": "Drama-based in the reader's-theatre sense: two characters argue their way to a decision almost entirely in dialogue, and the book is written to be performed aloud with a voice for each. Unlike mystery-missing-cat, where narration drives the events, here the narration only stages the exchanges and the conflict is carried by what the characters say to each other. It is not TBL - the reader never makes the decision, the characters do (compare bens-missing-backpack, where the reader chooses). Chosen to carry reported speech and the first conditional, both core B1 structures and both natural in an argument about what will happen next.",
  "grammarFeatures": [
    "First conditional for consequences (If we run away, nobody will know)",
    "Reported speech (Ayse said that she was afraid)",
    "Past simple narration around dialogue",
    "Modal \"have to\" for obligation"
  ],
  "introFunFacts": [
    "🎭 Two friends, one broken window. Read it out loud with a voice for Kerem and one for Ayse.",
    "🔀 Watch for sentences that start with 'If' - they tell you what WILL happen if something else happens first.",
    "🗣️ Some sentences say what a person said without using their exact words. Can you spot one?"
  ],
